    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "## Description\n\nWhen `cmdheight=0` (using noice.nvim), the resize animation in mini.animate corrupts `cmdheight` to an abnormally large value (e.g., 30).\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Set `cmdheight=0` (e.g., via noice.nvim)\n2. Open a vertical split (e.g., neo-tree sidebar)\n3. Run `:res 10` to shrink the current window height\n4. Observe that `cmdheight` jumps from 0 to ~30 and stays there\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n`cmdheight` should remain 0 after the resize animation completes.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\n`cmdheight` changes from 0 to a large value (30 in my case) and remains stuck.\nThe `apply_resize_state` function (animate.lua:1732) caches `cmdheight` **after** Neovim has already internally changed it to an incorrect value, then keeps restoring that wrong value on every animation frame.\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe fix for #270 added `cache_cmdheight` logic to preserve `cmdheight` during resize. However, it appears that Neovim internally changes `cmdheight` before `apply_resize_state` can cache the original value, so the cache captures the corrupted value instead.\n\nStack trace from `OptionSet` autocmd:\n\n```\n[cmdheight changed] 0 -> 30  (no Lua stack trace — changed at C level)\n[cmdheight changed] 30 -> 30\n  mini/animate.lua:1743 in apply_resize_state\n```\n\n## Workaround\n\nDisable the resize animation:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.animate').setup({\n  resize = { enable = false },\n})\n```\n\n## Environment\n\n- mini.nvim: v0.17.0\n- Neovim: v0.12.0\n- OS: macOS (Darwin 25.3.0)\n- Related plugins: noice.nvim (sets cmdheight=0)",
